### What is the Valid Parentheses problem?

Given a string containing only round, square, and curly brackets, determine whether every opening bracket is closed by a matching type in the correct nesting order. The LIFO discipline of a stack makes it the natural tool: the bracket that must close first is always the one most recently opened.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class ValidParentheses {
    public static boolean isValid(String s) {
        if (s.length() % 2 != 0) return false; // odd-length shortcut
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (pairs.containsValue(c)) {
                stack.push(c);
            } else if (pairs.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != pairs.get(c)) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isValid("()[]{}"));   // true
        System.out.println(isValid("({[]})"));   // true
        System.out.println(isValid("(]"));       // false
        System.out.println(isValid("((("));      // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
false
```

### Why does this problem use a stack?

Brackets must close in the reverse order they opened. Open round, then square, then curly means you must close curly first, then square, then round. This reverse ordering is LIFO, and a stack enforces it structurally. No other data structure gives you the most recently pushed element on every access.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class WhyStackForBrackets {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<String> approvals = new ArrayDeque<>();
        approvals.push("Loan opened");
        approvals.push("Document opened");
        approvals.push("Signature opened");

        // Must close in reverse order
        System.out.println(approvals.pop()); // Signature
        System.out.println(approvals.pop()); // Document
        System.out.println(approvals.pop()); // Loan
    }
}
```

```text
Signature opened
Document opened
Loan opened
```

### How does the algorithm work step by step?

Scan left to right. On each opener, push it. On each closer, check the stack is not empty and pop the top. Compare the popped opener to the expected match from the map. If anything fails, return false. After the scan, return `stack.isEmpty()`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class StepByStep {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "{[()]}";
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            char c = input.charAt(i);
            if (pairs.containsValue(c)) {
                stack.push(c);
                System.out.println("i=" + i + " push '" + c + "'");
            } else {
                char top = stack.pop();
                System.out.println("i=" + i + " pop '" + top + "' match '" + pairs.get(c) + "' -> " + (top == pairs.get(c)));
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Valid: " + stack.isEmpty());
    }
}
```

```text
i=0 push '{'
i=1 push '['
i=2 push '('
i=3 pop '(' match '(' -> true
i=4 pop '[' match '[' -> true
i=5 pop '{' match '{' -> true
Valid: true
```

### What are the two failure conditions?

First, a closer arrives when the stack is empty, meaning there is no opener to match. Second, the scan finishes with openers still on the stack, meaning some brackets were never closed. Both checks are essential. Missing the final emptiness check wrongly accepts strings like `"((("`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class FailureConditions {
    public static String check(String s) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (pairs.containsValue(c)) stack.push(c);
            else if (pairs.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty()) return "Fail: unmatched closer at " + i;
                if (stack.pop() != pairs.get(c)) return "Fail: type mismatch at " + i;
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty() ? "Valid" : "Fail: " + stack.size() + " unmatched openers";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(check(")("));     // unmatched closer
        System.out.println(check("((("));    // unmatched openers
        System.out.println(check("(]")); // type mismatch
    }
}
```

```text
Fail: unmatched closer at 0
Fail: 3 unmatched openers
Fail: type mismatch at 1
```

### What are the edge cases?

Empty string is valid. Odd-length string is always invalid. Single opener or single closer is invalid. Deeply nested valid strings and sequential valid pairs both pass.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class EdgeCasesDemo {
    static boolean isValid(String s) {
        if (s.length() % 2 != 0) return false;
        java.util.Deque<Character> st = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        var p = java.util.Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (p.containsValue(c)) st.push(c);
            else if (p.containsKey(c) && (st.isEmpty() || st.pop() != p.get(c))) return false;
        }
        return st.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + isValid(""));          // true
        System.out.println("Odd: " + isValid("(()")); // false
        System.out.println("Deep: " + isValid("(((())))")); // true
        System.out.println("Seq: " + isValid("()[]{}")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
Odd: false
Deep: true
Seq: true
```

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting the final `stack.isEmpty()` check is the most common bug: it wrongly accepts all-opener strings. Another mistake is not checking for an empty stack before popping, which throws `NoSuchElementException`. A third is comparing `Character` wrapper objects with `==` beyond the cached range; using `char` primitives avoids this.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class CommonBugs {
    // WRONG: missing final isEmpty check
    static boolean buggy(String s) {
        Deque<Character> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') st.push(c);
            else { if (st.isEmpty()) return false; st.pop(); }
        }
        return true; // BUG: should be st.isEmpty()
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Buggy accepts '(((': " + buggy("(((")); // true (wrong!)
    }
}
```

```text
Buggy accepts '(((': true
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At National Bank of India, fee formulas use nested brackets like `{(base + [tax * rate]) * discount}`. A parentheses validator runs before the expression parser. If the brackets are unbalanced, the formula is rejected and logged, protecting the calculation engine from evaluating corrupt expressions.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class FeeFormulaValidator {
    private static final Map<Character, Character> PAIRS =
            Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

    public static boolean validate(String formula) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : formula.toCharArray()) {
            if (PAIRS.containsValue(c)) stack.push(c);
            else if (PAIRS.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != PAIRS.get(c)) return false;
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(validate("{(base + [tax * rate]) * discount}")); // true
        System.out.println(validate("{(base + [tax * rate) * discount}")); // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### What about time and space complexity?

Time is O(n) because each character is visited once, pushed at most once, and popped at most once. Space is O(n) in the worst case where every character is an opener. In practice the stack only grows to the maximum nesting depth.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class Complexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Time: O(n) - single pass, each char pushed/popped at most once");
        System.out.println("Space: O(n) worst case - all openers like '(((((('");
        System.out.println("Best case: O(1) early exit on odd length or first mismatch");
    }
}
```

```text
Time: O(n) - single pass, each char pushed/popped at most once
Space: O(n) worst case - all openers like '(((((('
Best case: O(1) early exit on odd length or first mismatch
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

This technique extends directly to matching HTML/XML tags, validating nested expressions, and checking balanced quotes in parsers. The map-based matching is extensible: adding angle brackets or custom delimiters is a one-line change. Always prefer `ArrayDeque` over `Stack` because `Stack` extends `Vector` with unnecessary synchronization.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class ExtensibleMatcher {
    // Add custom delimiters with one extra map entry
    private static final Map<Character, Character> PAIRS = Map.of(
            ')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{', '>', '<'
    );

    public static boolean isValid(String s) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (PAIRS.containsValue(c)) stack.push(c);
            else if (PAIRS.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != PAIRS.get(c)) return false;
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isValid("<{[()]}>"));  // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
```
